The  command  attaches  an  instance of the efficient memory file
system to the global file system namespace.  The  parameter  only
exists for compatibility with the other mount commands and is ig‐
nored.   The  directory  specified by is converted to an absolute
path before use and its attributes (owner, group  and  mode)  are
inherited  unless  explicitly overridden by the options described
below.  The following options are supported: Specifies the  group
name  or  GID  of the root inode of the file system.  Defaults to
the mount point’s GID.  Specifies the mode (in octal notation) of
the root inode of the file system.  Defaults to the mount point’s
mode.  Specifies the maximum number of  nodes  available  to  the
file system.  If not specified, the file system chooses a reason‐
able  maximum  given its size at mount time, which can be limited
with Options are specified with a flag followed by a  comma‐sepa‐
rated  string  of options.  See the man page for possible options
and their meanings.  Specifies the  total  file  system  size  in
bytes.   If  zero is given (the default), the available amount of
memory (including main memory and swap space) will be used.  Note
that some memory is always reserved for the system and cannot  be
assigned  to  the  file  system.  The exact amount depends on the
available memory and details of the kernel memory usage, it might
even change slightly during runtime.  can alternatively be speci‐
fied as a percentage of the available system ram by using the no‐
tation where is a number between 1 and 100.  Similarly it can  be
specified  as  a  fraction  of  the available system ram by using
where is the divisor.  (Using and will result in the same limit.)
Specifies the user name or UID of the root inode of the file sys‐
tem.  Defaults to the mount point’s UID.  Every option  that  ac‐
cepts  a  numerical  value as its argument can take a trailing to
indicate bytes (the default), a to indicate kilobytes, a to indi‐
cate megabytes or a to indicate gigabytes.  Note that both lower‐
case and uppercase forms of these letters are allowed.  The  fol‐
